<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate the performance of DFlash on the popular open-source inference framework SGLang using Qwen3-4B, Qwen3-8B, and Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct. All experiments are conducted on a single B200 GPU with the FlashAttention-4 (FA4) backend. We enable Spec-v2 scheduling overlap to maximize achievable throughput.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Performance on Serving Frameworks ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DFlash: Block Diffusion for Flash Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>, DFlash consistently provides speedups across all three models over concurrency levels ranging from 1 to 32, achieving up to a 5.1<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup on Qwen3-8B. These results demonstrate the practical value of DFlash in real-world serving scenarios, where it can substantially reduce serving cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We report additional DFlash speedup results for more models and for vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> in <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Results on More Models and vLLM ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ DFlash: Block Diffusion for Flash Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Long Context Adaptation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that the base DFLash draft models trained on 4K context can adapt to longer context with minimal fine-tuning. We fine-tune the base Qwen3.5-27B draft model with 1.6K samples from LongAlign-10K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> for 3 epochs and test the performance on several datasets from LongBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
